package duration

// Parse reads a duration such as "90m".
func Parse(s string) (Duration, error) {
	unit := s[len(s)-1]
	// ...
}

content.text:
The bug is on the first line of `Parse`:

```go
unit := s[len(s)-1]
```

When `s` is empty, `len(s)-1` is `-1`, so the index is out of range. Go reports that as `index out of range [-1]`. The function never checks for an empty string before reading the last character.

The fix is an early return for empty input, before any indexing:

```go
func Parse(s string) (Duration, error) {
	if len(s) == 0 {
		return 0, fmt.Errorf("duration: empty string")
	}
	unit := s[len(s)-1]
	// ...
}
```

The file I read is only partial. The body after the first line is a `// ...` placeholder, and as shown it wouldn't compile, since `unit` is unused and there's no return. So this may not be the exact code your service runs. If the panic comes from a different copy of `Parse`, or from a vendored or versioned module, that is the file to check. You can confirm with `go list -m -f '{{.Dir}}' <module>` or by checking the stack trace's file and line.
